Lots of good suggestions have been made, e.g.: _do_
wear the good armor while your HPs and PWs are low,
wait until later to become a spelling buzzsaw
dressed only in a robe and an evil sneer.

One suggestion unmade so far: play ORCISH wizards,
at least until you become a wizard guiding guru.

Being able to eat kobolds and other poisonous monster
corpses promotes survival early in the game, removes
somewhat the constant threat of "Hungry, Weak,
Fainted, Starved", and an eventual cornathaum or
helm of brilliance later on can make up for the
orc racial INT limit of 16 when you really need to
be a spellcasting terror.

Another suggestion: practice up on the protection
racket (with any role) to gain melee-avoiding skills
that will improve your wizard survival rate.

A warning not yet seen: be careful with force bolt,
so as not to break nymph-carried mirrors and nerf
your luck. The thing about bad luck is you don't see
that it is what is really killing you when you're
dying, so avoiding it is a hard lesson to learn.

And yes, giant bats are _still_ a frequent
wizard-killer, even with all that: bats move fast,
hit hard, and appear out of nowhere.

So, put a pet between you and Batty-Kota. Even in
mid-room, dancing around a pet to keep it between
you and the bat can save your life while the pet
slowly chews the wings off the bat. This is a trick
the protection racket helps you practice, where
"never be in hitting range" is an _obvious_
behavior-of-merit.

